      "id": 1,
      "text": "Smithville began as a stagecoach stop built in 1787 by James Baremore.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://shorelocalnews.com/historic-smithville-how-one-couple-created-a-town/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In the 1960s, Fred and Ethel Noyes transformed the area into a historic attraction modeled after Colonial Williamsburg.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smithville%2C_Atlantic_County%2C_New_Jersey",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Noyes gathered and restored historic South Jersey buildings, turning them into shops and attractions in the Village.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smithville%2C_Atlantic_County%2C_New_Jersey",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Smithville sits 15 miles north of Atlantic City, inland from the boardwalk.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smithville%2C_Atlantic_County%2C_New_Jersey",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The original inn was abandoned sometime during the turn of the 20th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
